anna: quick triage thread — support saw multiple customers paste our 'retry' example and immediately hit 429s. opening to coordinate a tiny docs fix.
john: can you paste the failing response shape?
anna: from customer:
```
HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Retry-After: 5
{"error":"rate_limited"}
```
priya: likely culprits: (1) snippet uses old SDK keys (maxRetries, timeoutMs) and (2) prose encourages a fixed sleep which ignores Retry-After. both amplify the issue.
matt: agree. proposal: single focused PR to do three things: add a 1-line migration table (old->new), swap fixed-sleep example for a tiny backoff+jitter helper that honors Retry-After, and add a 4-item troubleshooting checklist at top. keep changes minimal.
priya: suggested migration rows: `maxRetries -> retryCount`, `timeoutMs -> requestTimeout`. add a note: "preferred keys in bold".
john: for the helper, keep it language-agnostic but include a minimal JS snippet showing honoring Retry-After. e.g.
```js
async function callWithRetry(fn, retries = 3) {
  for (let i = 0; i < retries; i++) {
    try { return await fn(); }
    catch (err) {
      const ra = err?.response?.headers?.['retry-after'];
      if (ra) await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Number(ra) * 1000));
      else await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Math.pow(2, i) * 100 + Math.random() * 100));
      if (i === retries - 1) throw err;
    }
  }
}
```
anna: +1. also add a one-line stream triage pattern for support: `stream aborted: requestTimeout=\\d+ms` so they can grep logs.
matt: add checklist at top: "If you see repeated 429s: 1) verify SDK param names (see migration table), 2) honor Retry-After header, 3) use exponential backoff with jitter, 4) for streams: handle partial payloads/aborts."
priya: docs-ci: i'll open PR with those edits and a single-line changelog blurb. will not change other prose.
